 { White to play, find the strongest sequence. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 Rfc8 ( 1... h6 2. h5 $1 Nh8 3. Rfc1 $16 { is equally bad, as the Black knight will struggle to re-enter the play. } ) 
2. h5 Nf8 3. h6 $16 { Correct! In order to avoid the knight from being left stranded on h8, Black was forced to allow White's pawn to march all the way to h6. As a result, the dark squares around Black's king get exposed and likely White will be able to set up a deadly attack in the coming moves. These flank-pawn attacks on knights that are on either b6/g6 for Black, or b3/g3 for White, are very typical and worth remembering as they can be positionally devastating in many situations. } 
