 { This position was reached in the game Penrose,J - Tal, M (1960) . Can you find the powerful sequence that White played in a game that he would go on to win against the then World Champion? } 19. { -KEY- } e5 $1 { A nasty pawn break in the centre of the board. } dxe5 20. f5 $1 { Correct! The pawn breaks serve to block the communication of Black's pieces along the e5 square as well as giving White attacking opportunities on the kingside plus a dangerous passed pawn. In case you would like to see how the game continued, the moves are included without annotation. } Bb7 21. Rad1 Ba8 22. Nce4 Na4 23. Bxa4 bxa4 24. fxg6 fxg6 25. Qf7+ Kh8 26. Nc5 Qa7 27. Qxd7 Qxd7 28. Nxd7 Rxb2 29. Nb6 Rb3 30. Nxc4 Rd8 31. d6 Rc3 32. Rc1 Rxc1 33. Rxc1 Bd5 34. Nb6 Bb3 35. Ne4 h6 36. d7 Bf8 37. Rc8 Be7 38. Bc5 Bh4 39. g3 { Black resigned. } 
